message.content:
I notice a bug in `load_omf_segdef`! For the 16-bit case (OMF_SEGDEF):

```c
if (record->size < 3 + off_add) {
    eprintf ("Invalid Segdef record (bad size)\n");
    return false;
}
ret->name_idx = omf_get_idx (buf + 6 + off_add, buf_size - 6 - off_add);
if (buf[3] & 2) {
    ret->size = UT16_MAX;
}
ret->size = r_read_le16 (buf + 4 + off_add);
```

If `buf[3] & 2` is true, `ret->size` is set to `UT16_MAX`, but then the next line `ret->size = r_read_le16(buf + 4 + off_add)` OVERWRITES it! The `UT16_MAX` assignment is useless because it's immediately overwritten. This is a logic bug but not a crash.

Wait, actually looking more carefully, there's no `else` before `ret->size = r_read_le16(...)`. So the code does:
1. If bit 2 is set, set size to UT16_MAX
2. Then ALWAYS set size to r_read_le16

This means the `UT16_MAX` is always overwritten. This is a logic bug but not a crash.

Let me look at the 32-bit case:
```c
if (buf[3] & 2) {
    ret->size = UT32_MAX;
} else {
    ret->size = r_read_le32 (buf + 4 + off_add);
}
```

This one has the `else`, so it's correct.

